  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>More be the ability of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>-atom of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> group to donate lone pair of electrons to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCl</mi></math> (dil.), more be the reactivity of amine.</p><p>In (B) two alkyl groups are attached to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> (having electron releasing nature) which increases the electron donating ability of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>-atom of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. In aqueous medium, secondary amine is most basic.&nbsp;Hence, it&nbsp;is most reactive.&nbsp;&nbsp;In (D), due to resonance, lone pair of electrons get delocalised and its basic strength is very less.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
